# The watchdog's cadence: once a minute.
#
# Faster than the health check's two minutes, and the reason is the window
# rather than the probe. The watchdog's default is to act on the Nth consecutive
# failure, and at a two-minute cadence three failures is six minutes of a machine
# with no resolver before anything is done about it; at one minute the same three
# failures are about two. The probe is one two-second UDP query to loopback and
# no transfer, so the cost of the cadence is a socket and a journal line.
#
# It is a separate timer from the health check rather than a second ExecStart
# line in mosdns-cdn-health.service, for a reason that is about what the two
# commands are: `verify-local` FAILS when the local resolver is not answering,
# and a service with two ExecStart lines and no SuccessExitStatus for that would
# be a failed unit every time a machine was in the state this watchdog exists to
# be in. Its own timer means its own exit status is its own story.
#
# Deliberately NOT Persistent, and that is the same decision as the health
# timer's: a calendar timer with Persistent=true replays every elapse the machine
# missed, and at a one-minute cadence a laptop that was closed for a day comes
# back and runs 1440 watchdog runs in a row. A watchdog that runs 1440 times
# after a resume is not a watchdog; it is a loop. A run that is a minute late is
# still a run.
#
# The first run is three minutes after boot, not immediately. At boot the router
# is still coming up -- the install starts it after dnscrypt-proxy, and a cold
# machine may still be settling -- so a probe before then fails by construction.
# The window would absorb it, but a first probe that is guaranteed to fail is not
# evidence, and it does not belong in the record that says when this machine
# stopped resolving. Three minutes is a judgement and not a measurement: it is
# the point after which a machine that is going to serve DNS normally is serving
# it on every release this package supports.
#
# To move the cadence, drop in over this timer and reload it -- the commands are
# the ones in mosdns-cdn-optimizer.timer, with this timer's name. The count and
# the elapsed window are not here: they are /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml, and moving
# them must not mean editing a unit file on a machine that may be the one with no
# resolver.

[Unit]
Description=One-minute resolver watchdog schedule
Documentation=man:mosdns-router(8)

[Timer]
OnBootSec=3min
OnUnitActiveSec=1min
Unit=mosdns-watchdog.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
